<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masnavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Masnavi-ye Ma'navi, Mathnawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Masnavi, or Masnavi-ye Ma'navi, is a long Persian poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Jalal al-Din Rumi</a>, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in the last years of his life. In six books of some 25,000 rhyming couplets, it teaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> doctrine through stories, fables and commentary on the Qur'an. It is one of the most influential works of Persian literature and of Islamic mysticism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masnavi.jpg" alt="Masnavi" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Jansoone (JoJan) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey.Konya049.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
